  "authoredSolutions": {
    "164": "For differentiability on \\(R\\), \\(f\\) must be continuous and have matching derivatives at \\(x=1\\). Continuity: \\(1+3+a = b+2 \\Rightarrow b = a+2\\). Left derivative \\(f'(x)=2x+3\\) gives \\(5\\) at \\(x=1\\); right derivative is \\(b\\), so \\(b=5\\) and \\(a=3\\). Thus \\(f(x)=x^{2}+3x+3\\) for \\(x\\le 1\\) and \\(5x+2\\) for \\(x>1\\). Then \\(\\int_{-2}^{2}f\\,dx=\\int_{-2}^{1}(x^{2}+3x+3)\\,dx+\\int_{1}^{2}(5x+2)\\,dx=\\frac{15}{2}+\\frac{19}{2}=17\\).",
    "168": "For the \\(\\log\\) term need \\(\\frac{2x+3}{4x^{2}+x-3}>0\\). Since \\(4x^{2}+x-3=(4x-3)(x+1)\\), a sign chart gives \\(x\\in\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},-1\\right)\\cup\\left(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty\\right)\\). For \\(\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2x-1}{x+2}\\right)\\) need \\(-1\\le\\frac{2x-1}{x+2}\\le 1\\), i.e. \\(\\frac{x-3}{x+2}\\le 0\\) and \\(\\frac{3x+1}{x+2}\\ge 0\\), giving \\(x\\in\\left[-\\frac{1}{3},3\\right]\\). The intersection is \\(\\left(\\frac{3}{4},3\\right]=(\\alpha,\\beta]\\), so \\(\\alpha=\\frac{3}{4},\\ \\beta=3\\) and \\(5\\beta-4\\alpha=15-3=12\\)."
  },
